{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Jacob dwelt in the land of his father's sojournings, in the land of Canaan.</b> It should have said 'And Jacob dwelt in the land of his father's dwelling,' or 'And Jacob sojourned in the land of his father's sojournings' — and furthermore, why do I need 'in the land of Canaan'? Rather, it is because Scripture indicts Jacob for seeking to dwell in this world with a settled dwelling, to be a permanent resident in this world in the place of his father's sojournings. For his father did not do thus; rather, he was in this world like a stranger and like a passing guest inclined to lodge for the night, because the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, ״גּוּר בָּאָרֶץ הַזֹּאת״ (Genesis 26:3). He used the language of ״גּוּר״, for HaShem did not want him to seek a permanent dwelling in this world. For one cannot say that He used the language of ״גּוּר״ only because at that time he dwelt in a land not his own, and one who dwells in a foreign land is called a ״גֵר״ — for regarding this He said 'in the land of Canaan.' And in that very land Isaac was a permanent resident (תּוֹשָׁב), for it was his, since Abraham said, ״גֵר וְתוֹשָׁב אָנֹכִי עִמָּכֶם״ (Genesis 23:4) — 'If you wish, I am a stranger; and if not, I will take it by law,' etc. If so, Isaac too was a permanent resident in the land of Canaan, for it is an inheritance to him; and what is this that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, ״גּוּר בָּאָרֶץ״? Rather, it is certain that He said this concerning the sojourning of this world — that he should not seek for himself a dwelling of tranquility even in his own land, like one who rents lodgings from a landlord (Rosh HaShanah 9a). And Jacob did not learn from him to do thus; therefore the anger of Joseph's episode burst upon him." "Another matter: it was already said to Abraham, ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״ (Genesis 15:13), and Jacob too has a share in the payment of this debt, yet he sought a dwelling of tranquility in the place of his father's sojournings. For from the time Isaac was born the sojourning began, and Abraham and Isaac held themselves as sojourners and were wandering and tossed about from journey to journey, and they would not acquire an inheritance of field or vineyard — and all this they did in order to pay off quickly the debt of ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״. Granted, Esau, who went off to another land — it is well that he said, 'I have no share in the gift of this land, and not in the payment of the debt,' as Rashi explained at the end of parashat Vayishlach on the verse ״וַיֵּלֶךְ אֶל אֶרֶץ״ (Genesis 36:6). But Jacob was in the land of Canaan, and he wanted to take a share in the gift of the land, and he did not want to pay the debt of ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״ — therefore the anger of Joseph's episode burst upon him. Therefore it says 'in the land of Canaan': had the dwelling of rest not been annulled from Jacob, those days would not have counted for him toward the total of four hundred years, and the End would inevitably have been delayed."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:13.908621437Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}